{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"examples","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Examples"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Want to skip to the implementation? Check out these Solid examples:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"../examples/column-resizing","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Column Resizing"}]}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"../examples/column-resizing-performant","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Performant Column Resizing"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use getters for reactive inputs such as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"data"},{"type":"text","value":" when passing Solid signals to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createTable"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"column-resizing-setup","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Column Resizing Setup"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Here's how you set up your table to use column resizing features. Column resizing depends on column sizing, so add "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"columnSizingFeature"},{"type":"text","value":" before "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"columnResizingFeature"},{"type":"text","value":". Adding the column resizing feature enables the related APIs."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import {\n  createTable,\n  tableFeatures,\n  columnSizingFeature,\n  columnResizingFeature,\n} from '@tanstack/solid-table'\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({\n  columnSizingFeature,\n  columnResizingFeature,\n})\n\nconst table = createTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  get data() {\n    return data()\n  },\n})","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"column-resizing-solid-guide","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Column Resizing (Solid) Guide"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack Table provides built-in column resizing state and APIs for implementing column resizing in your table UI with a variety of options for UX and performance."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Column resizing builds on column sizing. If you only need to define starting, minimum, or maximum widths, see the "},{"type":"link","href":"./column-sizing","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Column Sizing Guide"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"enable-column-resizing","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Enable Column Resizing"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"To use column resizing, add "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"columnSizingFeature"},{"type":"text","value":" and then "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"columnResizingFeature"},{"type":"text","value":" to your features. The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"column.getCanResize()"},{"type":"text","value":" API will return "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"true"},{"type":"text","value":" by default for all columns, but you can either disable column resizing for all columns with the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"enableColumnResizing"},{"type":"text","value":" table option, or disable column resizing on a per-column basis with the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"enableResizing"},{"type":"text","value":" column option."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import {\n  columnResizingFeature,\n  columnSizingFeature,\n  tableFeatures,\n  createTable,\n} from '@tanstack/solid-table'\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({\n  columnSizingFeature,\n  columnResizingFeature,\n})\n\nconst columns = [\n  {\n    accessorKey: 'id',\n    enableResizing: false, // disable resizing for just this column\n    size: 200, // starting column size\n  },\n  //...\n]\n\nconst table = createTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  data,\n})","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"column-resize-mode","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Column Resize Mode"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"By default, the column resize mode is set to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"\"onEnd\""},{"type":"text","value":". This means that the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"column.getSize()"},{"type":"text","value":" API will not return the new column size until the user has finished resizing (dragging) the column. Usually a small UI indicator will be displayed while the user is resizing the column."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"\"onEnd\""},{"type":"text","value":" default exists because immediate resize updates can be expensive in large or complex tables: every drag movement updates the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"columnSizing"},{"type":"text","value":" state, and anything that reads column widths recomputes. Solid's fine-grained reactivity helps here, since only the computations that actually read the sizing state re-run, but if every header and cell reads "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"column.getSize()"},{"type":"text","value":" directly, a complex table can still stutter during an "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"\"onChange\""},{"type":"text","value":" drag. The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"\"onEnd\""},{"type":"text","value":" mode sidesteps this by deferring the size update until the drag finishes."}]},{"type":"blockquote","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Advanced column resizing performance tips will be discussed "},{"type":"link","href":"#advanced-column-resizing-performance","children":[{"type":"text","value":"down below"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If you want to change the column resize mode to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"\"onChange\""},{"type":"text","value":" for immediate column resizing renders, you can do so with the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"columnResizeMode"},{"type":"text","value":" table option."}]},{"type":"code","value":"const table = createTable({\n  //...\n  columnResizeMode: 'onChange', // change column resize mode to \"onChange\"\n})","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"column-resize-direction","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Column Resize Direction"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"By default, TanStack Table assumes that the table markup is laid out in a left-to-right direction. For right-to-left layouts, you may need to change the column resize direction to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"\"rtl\""},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"code","value":"const table = createTable({\n  //...\n  columnResizeDirection: 'rtl', // change column resize direction to \"rtl\" for certain locales\n})","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"connect-column-resizing-apis-to-ui","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Connect Column Resizing APIs to UI"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"There are a few really handy APIs that you can use to hook up your column resizing drag interactions to your UI."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":4,"id":"column-size-apis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Column Size APIs"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"To apply the size of a column to the column head cells, data cells, or footer cells, you can use the following APIs:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"header.getSize()\ncolumn.getSize()\ncell.column.getSize()","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"How you apply these size styles to your markup is up to you, but it is pretty common to use either CSS variables or inline styles to apply the column sizes."}]},{"type":"code","value":"<th\n  key={header.id}\n  colSpan={header.colSpan}\n  style={{ width: `${header.getSize()}px` }}\n>","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Though, as discussed in the "},{"type":"link","href":"#advanced-column-resizing-performance","children":[{"type":"text","value":"advanced column resizing performance section"}]},{"type":"text","value":", you may want to consider using CSS variables to apply column sizes to your markup."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":4,"id":"column-resize-apis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Column Resize APIs"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack Table provides a pre-built event handler to make your drag interactions easy to implement. These event handlers are just convenience functions that call other internal APIs to update the column sizing state and re-render the table. Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"header.getResizeHandler()"},{"type":"text","value":" to connect to your column resize drag interactions, for both mouse and touch events."}]},{"type":"code","value":"<ColumnResizeHandle\n  onMouseDown={header.getResizeHandler()} // for desktop\n  onTouchStart={header.getResizeHandler()} // for mobile\n/>","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":4,"id":"column-resize-indicator-with-column-resizing-state","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Column Resize Indicator with Column Resizing State"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack Table keeps track of a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"columnResizing"},{"type":"text","value":" state object that you can use to render a column resize indicator UI."}]},{"type":"code","value":"<ColumnResizeIndicator\n  style={{\n    transform: header.column.getIsResizing()\n      ? `translateX(${table.atoms.columnResizing.get().deltaOffset ?? 0}px)`\n      : '',\n  }}\n/>","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Because the table's state atoms are backed by Solid signals, the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.atoms.columnResizing.get()"},{"type":"text","value":" read inside JSX is reactive and the indicator follows the drag. This is the same pattern the "},{"type":"link","href":"../examples/column-resizing","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Column Resizing example"}]},{"type":"text","value":" uses."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"columnResizing"},{"type":"text","value":" state stores transient drag information:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"type columnResizingState = {\n  columnSizingStart: Array<[string, number]>\n  deltaOffset: null | number\n  deltaPercentage: null | number\n  isResizingColumn: false | string\n  startOffset: null | number\n  startSize: null | number\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You rarely need to manage this transient drag state yourself, but if you do, the recommended v9 approach is an external atom passed to the table's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"atoms"},{"type":"text","value":" option. External atoms give you fine-grained subscriptions anywhere in your app, and other code can observe the resize state without going through the component that owns the table."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { createAtom, useSelector } from '@tanstack/solid-store'\nimport type { columnResizingState } from '@tanstack/solid-table'\n\nconst columnResizingAtom = createAtom<columnResizingState>({\n  columnSizingStart: [],\n  deltaOffset: null,\n  deltaPercentage: null,\n  isResizingColumn: false,\n  startOffset: null,\n  startSize: null,\n})\n\nconst columnResizing = useSelector(columnResizingAtom) // subscribe wherever it is needed\n\nconst table = createTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  data,\n  atoms: {\n    columnResizing: columnResizingAtom,\n  },\n})","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Alternatively, the v8-style "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"state.columnResizing"},{"type":"text","value":" plus "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onColumnResizingChange"},{"type":"text","value":" pattern is still supported with Solid signals. It can be convenient for simple integrations or when migrating v8 code, but it is less fine-grained than external atoms. See the "},{"type":"link","href":"./table-state","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Table State Guide"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for a deeper comparison."}]},{"type":"code","value":"const [columnResizing, setColumnResizing] = createSignal<columnResizingState>({\n  columnSizingStart: [],\n  deltaOffset: null,\n  deltaPercentage: null,\n  isResizingColumn: false,\n  startOffset: null,\n  startSize: null,\n})\n\nconst table = createTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  data,\n  state: {\n    get columnResizing() {\n      return columnResizing() // connect the signal back down to the table\n    },\n  },\n  onColumnResizingChange: setColumnResizing,\n})","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"column-resizing-apis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Column Resizing APIs"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"header.getResizeHandler()"},{"type":"text","value":" to connect mouse or touch events to the resizing logic. Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"column.getCanResize()"},{"type":"text","value":" to decide whether to render a resize handle, and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"column.getIsResizing()"},{"type":"text","value":" to render active resizing UI."}]},{"type":"code","value":"header.getResizeHandler()\ncolumn.getCanResize()\ncolumn.getIsResizing()","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The table instance exposes APIs for the transient resize state through "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.setColumnResizing"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"code","value":"table.setColumnResizing((old) => ({\n  ...old,\n  deltaOffset: 12,\n}))\n\ntable.resetHeaderSizeInfo()\ntable.resetHeaderSizeInfo(true)","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"advanced-column-resizing-performance","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Advanced Column Resizing Performance"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Solid's fine-grained reactivity means you do not have to fight whole-component re-renders the way React users do. But if every header and data cell reads "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"column.getSize()"},{"type":"text","value":" directly, an "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"\"onChange\""},{"type":"text","value":" drag still recomputes a lot per frame. The "},{"type":"link","href":"../examples/column-resizing-performant","children":[{"type":"text","value":"performant column resizing example"}]},{"type":"text","value":" shows how to reduce a drag to a single reactive update."}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Compute all column widths in one "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createMemo"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"text","value":" Map each header and column id to a CSS variable value, return the whole style object, and bind it once on the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"<table>"},{"type":"text","value":" element ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"<table style={tableStyle()}>"},{"type":"text","value":"). "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"header.getSize()"},{"type":"text","value":" reads the signal-backed "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"columnSizing"},{"type":"text","value":" atom, so a drag re-runs only this one memo and its single style binding."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reference the variables in cell styles"}]},{"type":"text","value":" ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"width: calc(var(--col-firstName-size) * 1px)"},{"type":"text","value":") so the browser applies new widths without the cell JSX re-executing."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Because nothing in the table body reads resize state, no body memoization is needed; the resizer's own highlight is its own fine-grained binding and updates on its own."}]}],"headings":[{"id":"examples","text":"Examples","level":2},{"id":"column-resizing-setup","text":"Column Resizing Setup","level":3},{"id":"column-resizing-solid-guide","text":"Column Resizing (Solid) Guide","level":2},{"id":"enable-column-resizing","text":"Enable Column Resizing","level":3},{"id":"column-resize-mode","text":"Column Resize Mode","level":3},{"id":"column-resize-direction","text":"Column Resize Direction","level":3},{"id":"connect-column-resizing-apis-to-ui","text":"Connect Column Resizing APIs to UI","level":3},{"id":"column-size-apis","text":"Column Size APIs","level":4},{"id":"column-resize-apis","text":"Column Resize APIs","level":4},{"id":"column-resize-indicator-with-column-resizing-state","text":"Column Resize Indicator with Column Resizing State","level":4},{"id":"column-resizing-apis","text":"Column Resizing APIs","level":3},{"id":"advanced-column-resizing-performance","text":"Advanced Column Resizing Performance","level":3}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\n## Examples\n\nWant to skip to the implementation? Check out these Solid examples:\n\n- [Column Resizing](../examples/column-resizing)\n- [Performant Column Resizing](../examples/column-resizing-performant)\n\nUse getters for reactive inputs such as `data` when passing Solid signals to `createTable`.\n\n### Column Resizing Setup\n\nHere's how you set up your table to use column resizing features. Column resizing depends on column sizing, so add `columnSizingFeature` before `columnResizingFeature`. Adding the column resizing feature enables the related APIs.\n\n```tsx\nimport {\n  createTable,\n  tableFeatures,\n  columnSizingFeature,\n  columnResizingFeature,\n} from '@tanstack/solid-table'\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({\n  columnSizingFeature,\n  columnResizingFeature,\n})\n\nconst table = createTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  get data() {\n    return data()\n  },\n})\n```\n\n## Column Resizing (Solid) Guide\n\nTanStack Table provides built-in column resizing state and APIs for implementing column resizing in your table UI with a variety of options for UX and performance.\n\nColumn resizing builds on column sizing. If you only need to define starting, minimum, or maximum widths, see the [Column Sizing Guide](./column-sizing).\n\n### Enable Column Resizing\n\nTo use column resizing, add `columnSizingFeature` and then `columnResizingFeature` to your features. The `column.getCanResize()` API will return `true` by default for all columns, but you can either disable column resizing for all columns with the `enableColumnResizing` table option, or disable column resizing on a per-column basis with the `enableResizing` column option.\n\n```tsx\nimport {\n  columnResizingFeature,\n  columnSizingFeature,\n  tableFeatures,\n  createTable,\n} from '@tanstack/solid-table'\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({\n  columnSizingFeature,\n  columnResizingFeature,\n})\n\nconst columns = [\n  {\n    accessorKey: 'id',\n    enableResizing: false, // disable resizing for just this column\n    size: 200, // starting column size\n  },\n  //...\n]\n\nconst table = createTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  data,\n})\n```\n\n### Column Resize Mode\n\nBy default, the column resize mode is set to `\"onEnd\"`. This means that the `column.getSize()` API will not return the new column size until the user has finished resizing (dragging) the column. Usually a small UI indicator will be displayed while the user is resizing the column.\n\nThe `\"onEnd\"` default exists because immediate resize updates can be expensive in large or complex tables: every drag movement updates the `columnSizing` state, and anything that reads column widths recomputes. Solid's fine-grained reactivity helps here, since only the computations that actually read the sizing state re-run, but if every header and cell reads `column.getSize()` directly, a complex table can still stutter during an `\"onChange\"` drag. The `\"onEnd\"` mode sidesteps this by deferring the size update until the drag finishes.\n\n> Advanced column resizing performance tips will be discussed [down below](#advanced-column-resizing-performance).\n\nIf you want to change the column resize mode to `\"onChange\"` for immediate column resizing renders, you can do so with the `columnResizeMode` table option.\n\n```tsx\nconst table = createTable({\n  //...\n  columnResizeMode: 'onChange', // change column resize mode to \"onChange\"\n})\n```\n\n### Column Resize Direction\n\nBy default, TanStack Table assumes that the table markup is laid out in a left-to-right direction. For right-to-left layouts, you may need to change the column resize direction to `\"rtl\"`.\n\n```tsx\nconst table = createTable({\n  //...\n  columnResizeDirection: 'rtl', // change column resize direction to \"rtl\" for certain locales\n})\n```\n\n### Connect Column Resizing APIs to UI\n\nThere are a few really handy APIs that you can use to hook up your column resizing drag interactions to your UI.\n\n#### Column Size APIs\n\nTo apply the size of a column to the column head cells, data cells, or footer cells, you can use the following APIs:\n\n```ts\nheader.getSize()\ncolumn.getSize()\ncell.column.getSize()\n```\n\nHow you apply these size styles to your markup is up to you, but it is pretty common to use either CSS variables or inline styles to apply the column sizes.\n\n```tsx\n<th\n  key={header.id}\n  colSpan={header.colSpan}\n  style={{ width: `${header.getSize()}px` }}\n>\n```\n\nThough, as discussed in the [advanced column resizing performance section](#advanced-column-resizing-performance), you may want to consider using CSS variables to apply column sizes to your markup.\n\n#### Column Resize APIs\n\nTanStack Table provides a pre-built event handler to make your drag interactions easy to implement. These event handlers are just convenience functions that call other internal APIs to update the column sizing state and re-render the table. Use `header.getResizeHandler()` to connect to your column resize drag interactions, for both mouse and touch events.\n\n```tsx\n<ColumnResizeHandle\n  onMouseDown={header.getResizeHandler()} // for desktop\n  onTouchStart={header.getResizeHandler()} // for mobile\n/>\n```\n\n#### Column Resize Indicator with Column Resizing State\n\nTanStack Table keeps track of a `columnResizing` state object that you can use to render a column resize indicator UI.\n\n```tsx\n<ColumnResizeIndicator\n  style={{\n    transform: header.column.getIsResizing()\n      ? `translateX(${table.atoms.columnResizing.get().deltaOffset ?? 0}px)`\n      : '',\n  }}\n/>\n```\n\nBecause the table's state atoms are backed by Solid signals, the `table.atoms.columnResizing.get()` read inside JSX is reactive and the indicator follows the drag. This is the same pattern the [Column Resizing example](../examples/column-resizing) uses.\n\nThe `columnResizing` state stores transient drag information:\n\n```tsx\ntype columnResizingState = {\n  columnSizingStart: Array<[string, number]>\n  deltaOffset: null | number\n  deltaPercentage: null | number\n  isResizingColumn: false | string\n  startOffset: null | number\n  startSize: null | number\n}\n```\n\nYou rarely need to manage this transient drag state yourself, but if you do, the recommended v9 approach is an external atom passed to the table's `atoms` option. External atoms give you fine-grained subscriptions anywhere in your app, and other code can observe the resize state without going through the component that owns the table.\n\n```tsx\nimport { createAtom, useSelector } from '@tanstack/solid-store'\nimport type { columnResizingState } from '@tanstack/solid-table'\n\nconst columnResizingAtom = createAtom<columnResizingState>({\n  columnSizingStart: [],\n  deltaOffset: null,\n  deltaPercentage: null,\n  isResizingColumn: false,\n  startOffset: null,\n  startSize: null,\n})\n\nconst columnResizing = useSelector(columnResizingAtom) // subscribe wherever it is needed\n\nconst table = createTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  data,\n  atoms: {\n    columnResizing: columnResizingAtom,\n  },\n})\n```\n\nAlternatively, the v8-style `state.columnResizing` plus `onColumnResizingChange` pattern is still supported with Solid signals. It can be convenient for simple integrations or when migrating v8 code, but it is less fine-grained than external atoms. See the [Table State Guide](./table-state) for a deeper comparison.\n\n```tsx\nconst [columnResizing, setColumnResizing] = createSignal<columnResizingState>({\n  columnSizingStart: [],\n  deltaOffset: null,\n  deltaPercentage: null,\n  isResizingColumn: false,\n  startOffset: null,\n  startSize: null,\n})\n\nconst table = createTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  data,\n  state: {\n    get columnResizing() {\n      return columnResizing() // connect the signal back down to the table\n    },\n  },\n  onColumnResizingChange: setColumnResizing,\n})\n```\n\n### Column Resizing APIs\n\nUse `header.getResizeHandler()` to connect mouse or touch events to the resizing logic. Use `column.getCanResize()` to decide whether to render a resize handle, and `column.getIsResizing()` to render active resizing UI.\n\n```tsx\nheader.getResizeHandler()\ncolumn.getCanResize()\ncolumn.getIsResizing()\n```\n\nThe table instance exposes APIs for the transient resize state through `table.setColumnResizing`.\n\n```tsx\ntable.setColumnResizing((old) => ({\n  ...old,\n  deltaOffset: 12,\n}))\n\ntable.resetHeaderSizeInfo()\ntable.resetHeaderSizeInfo(true)\n```\n\n### Advanced Column Resizing Performance\n\nSolid's fine-grained reactivity means you do not have to fight whole-component re-renders the way React users do. But if every header and data cell reads `column.getSize()` directly, an `\"onChange\"` drag still recomputes a lot per frame. The [performant column resizing example](../examples/column-resizing-performant) shows how to reduce a drag to a single reactive update.\n\n1. **Compute all column widths in one `createMemo`.** Map each header and column id to a CSS variable value, return the whole style object, and bind it once on the `<table>` element (`<table style={tableStyle()}>`). `header.getSize()` reads the signal-backed `columnSizing` atom, so a drag re-runs only this one memo and its single style binding.\n2. **Reference the variables in cell styles** (`width: calc(var(--col-firstName-size) * 1px)`) so the browser applies new widths without the cell JSX re-executing.\n\nBecause nothing in the table body reads resize state, no body memoization is needed; the resizer's own highlight is its own fine-grained binding and updates on its own.\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: Column Resizing (Solid) Guide\n---\n\n## Examples\n\nWant to skip to the implementation? Check out these Solid examples:\n\n- [Column Resizing](../examples/column-resizing)\n- [Performant Column Resizing](../examples/column-resizing-performant)\n\nUse getters for reactive inputs such as `data` when passing Solid signals to `createTable`.\n\n### Column Resizing Setup\n\nHere's how you set up your table to use column resizing features. Column resizing depends on column sizing, so add `columnSizingFeature` before `columnResizingFeature`. Adding the column resizing feature enables the related APIs.\n\n```tsx\nimport {\n  createTable,\n  tableFeatures,\n  columnSizingFeature,\n  columnResizingFeature,\n} from '@tanstack/solid-table'\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({\n  columnSizingFeature,\n  columnResizingFeature,\n})\n\nconst table = createTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  get data() {\n    return data()\n  },\n})\n```\n\n## Column Resizing (Solid) Guide\n\nTanStack Table provides built-in column resizing state and APIs for implementing column resizing in your table UI with a variety of options for UX and performance.\n\nColumn resizing builds on column sizing. If you only need to define starting, minimum, or maximum widths, see the [Column Sizing Guide](./column-sizing).\n\n### Enable Column Resizing\n\nTo use column resizing, add `columnSizingFeature` and then `columnResizingFeature` to your features. The `column.getCanResize()` API will return `true` by default for all columns, but you can either disable column resizing for all columns with the `enableColumnResizing` table option, or disable column resizing on a per-column basis with the `enableResizing` column option.\n\n```tsx\nimport {\n  columnResizingFeature,\n  columnSizingFeature,\n  tableFeatures,\n  createTable,\n} from '@tanstack/solid-table'\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({\n  columnSizingFeature,\n  columnResizingFeature,\n})\n\nconst columns = [\n  {\n    accessorKey: 'id',\n    enableResizing: false, // disable resizing for just this column\n    size: 200, // starting column size\n  },\n  //...\n]\n\nconst table = createTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  data,\n})\n```\n\n### Column Resize Mode\n\nBy default, the column resize mode is set to `\"onEnd\"`. This means that the `column.getSize()` API will not return the new column size until the user has finished resizing (dragging) the column. Usually a small UI indicator will be displayed while the user is resizing the column.\n\nThe `\"onEnd\"` default exists because immediate resize updates can be expensive in large or complex tables: every drag movement updates the `columnSizing` state, and anything that reads column widths recomputes. Solid's fine-grained reactivity helps here, since only the computations that actually read the sizing state re-run, but if every header and cell reads `column.getSize()` directly, a complex table can still stutter during an `\"onChange\"` drag. The `\"onEnd\"` mode sidesteps this by deferring the size update until the drag finishes.\n\n> Advanced column resizing performance tips will be discussed [down below](#advanced-column-resizing-performance).\n\nIf you want to change the column resize mode to `\"onChange\"` for immediate column resizing renders, you can do so with the `columnResizeMode` table option.\n\n```tsx\nconst table = createTable({\n  //...\n  columnResizeMode: 'onChange', // change column resize mode to \"onChange\"\n})\n```\n\n### Column Resize Direction\n\nBy default, TanStack Table assumes that the table markup is laid out in a left-to-right direction. For right-to-left layouts, you may need to change the column resize direction to `\"rtl\"`.\n\n```tsx\nconst table = createTable({\n  //...\n  columnResizeDirection: 'rtl', // change column resize direction to \"rtl\" for certain locales\n})\n```\n\n### Connect Column Resizing APIs to UI\n\nThere are a few really handy APIs that you can use to hook up your column resizing drag interactions to your UI.\n\n#### Column Size APIs\n\nTo apply the size of a column to the column head cells, data cells, or footer cells, you can use the following APIs:\n\n```ts\nheader.getSize()\ncolumn.getSize()\ncell.column.getSize()\n```\n\nHow you apply these size styles to your markup is up to you, but it is pretty common to use either CSS variables or inline styles to apply the column sizes.\n\n```tsx\n<th\n  key={header.id}\n  colSpan={header.colSpan}\n  style={{ width: `${header.getSize()}px` }}\n>\n```\n\nThough, as discussed in the [advanced column resizing performance section](#advanced-column-resizing-performance), you may want to consider using CSS variables to apply column sizes to your markup.\n\n#### Column Resize APIs\n\nTanStack Table provides a pre-built event handler to make your drag interactions easy to implement. These event handlers are just convenience functions that call other internal APIs to update the column sizing state and re-render the table. Use `header.getResizeHandler()` to connect to your column resize drag interactions, for both mouse and touch events.\n\n```tsx\n<ColumnResizeHandle\n  onMouseDown={header.getResizeHandler()} // for desktop\n  onTouchStart={header.getResizeHandler()} // for mobile\n/>\n```\n\n#### Column Resize Indicator with Column Resizing State\n\nTanStack Table keeps track of a `columnResizing` state object that you can use to render a column resize indicator UI.\n\n```tsx\n<ColumnResizeIndicator\n  style={{\n    transform: header.column.getIsResizing()\n      ? `translateX(${table.atoms.columnResizing.get().deltaOffset ?? 0}px)`\n      : '',\n  }}\n/>\n```\n\nBecause the table's state atoms are backed by Solid signals, the `table.atoms.columnResizing.get()` read inside JSX is reactive and the indicator follows the drag. This is the same pattern the [Column Resizing example](../examples/column-resizing) uses.\n\nThe `columnResizing` state stores transient drag information:\n\n```tsx\ntype columnResizingState = {\n  columnSizingStart: Array<[string, number]>\n  deltaOffset: null | number\n  deltaPercentage: null | number\n  isResizingColumn: false | string\n  startOffset: null | number\n  startSize: null | number\n}\n```\n\nYou rarely need to manage this transient drag state yourself, but if you do, the recommended v9 approach is an external atom passed to the table's `atoms` option. External atoms give you fine-grained subscriptions anywhere in your app, and other code can observe the resize state without going through the component that owns the table.\n\n```tsx\nimport { createAtom, useSelector } from '@tanstack/solid-store'\nimport type { columnResizingState } from '@tanstack/solid-table'\n\nconst columnResizingAtom = createAtom<columnResizingState>({\n  columnSizingStart: [],\n  deltaOffset: null,\n  deltaPercentage: null,\n  isResizingColumn: false,\n  startOffset: null,\n  startSize: null,\n})\n\nconst columnResizing = useSelector(columnResizingAtom) // subscribe wherever it is needed\n\nconst table = createTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  data,\n  atoms: {\n    columnResizing: columnResizingAtom,\n  },\n})\n```\n\nAlternatively, the v8-style `state.columnResizing` plus `onColumnResizingChange` pattern is still supported with Solid signals. It can be convenient for simple integrations or when migrating v8 code, but it is less fine-grained than external atoms. See the [Table State Guide](./table-state) for a deeper comparison.\n\n```tsx\nconst [columnResizing, setColumnResizing] = createSignal<columnResizingState>({\n  columnSizingStart: [],\n  deltaOffset: null,\n  deltaPercentage: null,\n  isResizingColumn: false,\n  startOffset: null,\n  startSize: null,\n})\n\nconst table = createTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  data,\n  state: {\n    get columnResizing() {\n      return columnResizing() // connect the signal back down to the table\n    },\n  },\n  onColumnResizingChange: setColumnResizing,\n})\n```\n\n### Column Resizing APIs\n\nUse `header.getResizeHandler()` to connect mouse or touch events to the resizing logic. Use `column.getCanResize()` to decide whether to render a resize handle, and `column.getIsResizing()` to render active resizing UI.\n\n```tsx\nheader.getResizeHandler()\ncolumn.getCanResize()\ncolumn.getIsResizing()\n```\n\nThe table instance exposes APIs for the transient resize state through `table.setColumnResizing`.\n\n```tsx\ntable.setColumnResizing((old) => ({\n  ...old,\n  deltaOffset: 12,\n}))\n\ntable.resetHeaderSizeInfo()\ntable.resetHeaderSizeInfo(true)\n```\n\n### Advanced Column Resizing Performance\n\nSolid's fine-grained reactivity means you do not have to fight whole-component re-renders the way React users do. But if every header and data cell reads `column.getSize()` directly, an `\"onChange\"` drag still recomputes a lot per frame. The [performant column resizing example](../examples/column-resizing-performant) shows how to reduce a drag to a single reactive update.\n\n1. **Compute all column widths in one `createMemo`.** Map each header and column id to a CSS variable value, return the whole style object, and bind it once on the `<table>` element (`<table style={tableStyle()}>`). `header.getSize()` reads the signal-backed `columnSizing` atom, so a drag re-runs only this one memo and its single style binding.\n2. **Reference the variables in cell styles** (`width: calc(var(--col-firstName-size) * 1px)`) so the browser applies new widths without the cell JSX re-executing.\n\nBecause nothing in the table body reads resize state, no body memoization is needed; the resizer's own highlight is its own fine-grained binding and updates on its own.\n"}
